		<link>https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Ekemini]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 07:41:00 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Agentic AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI hardware]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[artificial intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[BlackRock]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Carpenters]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Data Centers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Electricians]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Meta]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://ventureburn.com/?p=203849</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Behind every chatbot is a building with heavy machinery, power lines, and cooling units. That physical side is hitting a wall, which is why thousands of electricians and carpenters are</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal">Meta and Google Commit $265 Million on Electricians, Carpenters Building Data Centers</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Behind every chatbot is a building with heavy machinery, power lines, and cooling units. That physical side is hitting a wall, which is why thousands of electricians and carpenters are recruited to build AI data centers across the country today.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">If someone isn’t on-site pulling cable, running pipe, and building walls, the tech companies can’t launch a single new model. As a result, the big names building data centers are collectively committing more than $265 million on electricians, carpenters, and other construction workers.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Why Tech Is Tilting Towards Skilled Trades</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">It takes much more to build a data center. It requires an insane amount of electricity and industrial cooling loops to keep the servers from overheating.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Contractors often feel pressured to finish these builds quickly. As a result, demand for skilled trade labor has increased over the last few years. In high-density build areas, it’s no surprise seeing electricians work 10-hour shifts, seven days a week, just to keep things moving.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Why Workers Are Switching</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_203851" style="width: 1034px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-203851" class="size-large wp-image-203851" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Electrician-working-on-a-data-center-1024x683.jpg" alt="An Electricians working on electrical parts inside a massive AI data center construction site" width="1024" height="683" /><p id="caption-attachment-203851" class="wp-caption-text">Data Center Engineer</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">At the end of the day, people go where the money is. Industrial data center work pays way more than standard home-building or local commercial jobs. Electricians putting in heavy overtime on these sites are pulling down hundreds of thousands of dollars every year.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">That kind of pay shifts the whole job market. Experienced workers are walking away from home-building sites to jump onto data center contracts. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">At the same time, folks leaving jobs in retail or teaching are signing up for trade apprenticeships. The side effect? Local homebuilders are left scrambling for help, putting everyday housing projects behind schedule.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/freehand-raises-75-million-ai-agents-supply-chain/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Freehand Raises $75M for AI Agents in Supply Chains</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Who’s Really Building the Tech World?</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Capital and software code aren’t the main things slowing tech companies down right now, it’s having enough qualified people to do the physical work. Whenever local red tape or construction pauses hit, trade workers take the financial brunt of it first. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">To make sure they don’t run out of labor, major tech firms are dropping real money straight into community colleges and local union halls to train the next wave of workers themselves. The numbers backing this surge tell the whole story. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Demand for skilled trade positions has grown nearly 30% over the last three years, outpacing typical commercial roles. Specialized data center labor carries a 32% pay premium, with electricians logging heavy overtime to bring home between $114,000 and $280,000 annually in major tech hubs like Texas and Virginia. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Meanwhile, the broader U.S. construction industry is sitting on a severe gap of roughly 439,000 workers. Tech companies are stepping in directly, committing hundreds of millions of dollars to local trade programs to plug the deficit.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">To close the gap, tech giants are committing massive capital to short-term training. Meta </span><a href="https://about.fb.com/news/2026/06/americas-workforce-academy-free-skilled-trade-training/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">launched</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> its $115 million America’s Workforce Academy while Google pledged $10 million to the Electrical Training Alliance. Meanwhile, applications for commercial electrical apprenticeships surged over 70% between 2022 and 2024. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The NY Times </span><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/29/business/economy/data-center-electricians-training.html" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">reported</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> that big tech has committed $265 million to recruit and train electricians, carpenters, and other construction workers building data centers. As one industry report put it, “A finished data center takes way fewer workers to run than to build.&#8221; AI may be code, but data centers are built by hand. </span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/qureight-raises-20m-series-b-ai-lung-heart-clinical-trials/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Qureight Raises $20M for AI-powered Lung and Heart Clinical Trials</i></b></a></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal">Meta and Google Commit $265 Million on Electricians, Carpenters Building Data Centers</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
